WEBVTT

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.120
Local large-language-model infrastructure is the hardware, runtime software, storage,

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.150
interfaces, controls,

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.000
and operating practices used to run model inference on systems you control.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.175
The model may live on a laptop, workstation, gaming PC, mini PC, server,

00:00:14.175 --> 00:00:16.000
or managed private environment.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.917
Local inference sends prompts to a model running in that environment.

00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:23.590
A cloud API sends requests to an external provider under its service, security, pricing,

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.000
and data-handling model.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.180
Hybrid designs may route different workloads according to sensitivity, quality, cost,

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:30.255
or availability.

00:00:30.255 --> 00:00:33.000
Hardware choices include CPUs, discrete GPUs,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.385
unified-memory systems such as Apple Silicon, system memory, storage, networking, power,

00:00:37.385 --> 00:00:38.245
and cooling.

00:00:38.245 --> 00:00:42.950
Software choices include model formats, runtimes, drivers, user interfaces, local APIs,

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.985
monitoring, and access control.

00:00:44.985 --> 00:00:49.510
Local operation can improve data control, offline capability, experimentation,

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.000
customization, and cost predictability for steady workloads.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.510
It also transfers responsibility for hardware limits, software updates, security, backups,

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.230
availability, and model governance to the operator.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:01.980
Performance depends on the complete configuration.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.390
Model architecture, parameter count, quantization, context length, prompt size,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.000
batch behavior, concurrency, runtime, acceleration backend,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000
and cooling can matter as much as the device name.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.980
The right question is not whether local is always better.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.500
It is whether a measured local system meets the task's quality, privacy, latency,

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.400
throughput, cost,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.000
and operational requirements more effectively than available alternatives.
